## File 2: `src/errors.js` — Custom Error Classes

```javascript
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * SECTION: CUSTOM ERROR CLASSES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * WHY custom error classes instead of plain Error objects?
 *   - They carry structured metadata (statusCode, isOperational) that middleware
 *     can use to send appropriate HTTP responses without guessing.
 *   - "isOperational" distinguishes programmer bugs (not our fault to catch)
 *     from expected errors like "resource not found" (our fault to handle).
 *   - It makes error-handling middleware DRY — one place handles all types.
 *
 * Design choice: We create a hierarchy so we can catch broad categories in
 * middleware while still being specific in route handlers.
 */

/**
 * Base application error class.
 * All custom errors extend this so we can identify them uniformly.
 */
class AppError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Human-readable error description.
   * @param {number} statusCode - HTTP status code (e.g., 404, 400).
   * @param {boolean} [isOperational=true] - Whether this is an expected error.
   */
  constructor(message, statusCode, isOperational = true) {
    super(message);
    // Capture the stack trace at the point of instantiation for debugging.
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = isOperational;
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

/**
 * Error thrown when a requested resource does not exist.
 * HTTP convention: 404 Not Found.
 */
class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource, id) {
    // e.g., "Book with id abc123 not found"
    super(`${resource} with id ${id} not found`, 404);
  }
}

/**
 * Error thrown when client input fails validation.
 * HTTP convention: 400 Bad Request.
 */
class ValidationError extends AppError {
  /**
   * @param {string[]} messages - Array of human-readable validation messages.
   */
  constructor(messages) {
    super('Validation failed', 400);
    // Attach the detailed messages so the response can include them.
    this.messages = messages;
  }
}

/**
 * Error thrown when a book already exists with the same unique field.
 * HTTP convention: 409 Conflict.
 */
class ConflictError extends AppError {
  constructor(field, value) {
    super(`${field} "${value}" already exists`, 409);
  }
}

module.exports = { AppError, NotFoundError, ValidationError, ConflictError };
```

**Key takeaway:** Custom error classes let middleware make decisions based on `statusCode` and `isOperational` rather than parsing message strings. This is the foundation of clean, maintainable error handling.

