## File 6: `src/app.js` — Application Entry Point

```javascript
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * SECTION: APPLICATION ENTRY POINT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * WHY this structure?
 *   - We require Express, create the app instance, then configure middleware
 *     BEFORE mounting routes. This ensures every request passes through our
 *     logging and error-handling layers.
 *   - Routes are mounted at specific paths (/api/books, /api/stats) so we can
 *     add more API versions or namespaces later without conflict.
 *   - The root path (/) returns a friendly welcome message — useful for quick
 *     health checks during development.
 * ============================================================================
 */

const express = require('express');
const { requestLogger, errorHandler } = require('./middleware');
const bookRoutes = require('./routes/books');
const statsRoutes = require('./routes/stats');

// Create the Express application instance.
const app = express();

// ============================================================================
// MIDDLEWARE CONFIGURATION
// ============================================================================
// Order matters! Middleware runs top-to-bottom in the order it's added.

// 1. JSON body parser — converts incoming JSON request bodies into req.body objects.
//    Without this, POST/PUT bodies would be raw strings.
app.use(express.json());

// 2. Request logger — logs every request for visibility and debugging.
app.use(requestLogger);

// ============================================================================
// ROUTE MOUNTING
// ============================================================================
// All API routes are namespaced under /api so we can add non-API routes later.

// Book CRUD: GET /api/books, GET /api/books/:id, POST /api/books, etc.
app.use('/api/books', bookRoutes);

// Statistics: GET /api/stats
app.use('/api/stats', statsRoutes);

// Root endpoint — friendly welcome message for developers.
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    success: true,
    message: '📚 Book REST API is running!',
    endpoints: {
      books: 'GET /api/books, GET /api/books/:id, POST /api/books, PUT /api/books/:id, DELETE /api/books/:id',
      stats: 'GET /api/stats',
    },
  });
});

// ============================================================================
// 404 HANDLER FOR UNRECOGNIZED ROUTES
// ============================================================================
// If no route matches, send a 404. This must come AFTER all route definitions.
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({
    success: false,
    error: { message: `Route ${req.method} ${req.originalUrl} not found` },
  });
});

// ============================================================================
// GLOBAL ERROR HANDLER (MUST BE LAST)
// ============================================================================
// Express requires exactly 4 parameters to identify this as error middleware.
app.use(errorHandler);

// ============================================================================
// SERVER STARTUP
// ============================================================================
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`✅ Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
  console.log(`📖 API docs: http://localhost:${PORT}/api`);
});

module.exports = app;
```

**Why mount routes after middleware?** Middleware like `express.json()` must run before route handlers that read `req.body`. The order ensures the body is parsed before any route tries to access it.

**Why the 404 catch-all at the end?** It catches requests to undefined routes. Placing it after all route definitions ensures legitimate routes get priority.

