## Summary of Design Decisions

| Decision | Choice | Rationale |
|----------|--------|-----------|
| Storage | In-memory array | No setup required; focuses on API patterns |
| Error handling | Custom classes + middleware | DRY, structured, consistent responses |
| Route organization | Separate files per resource | Single responsibility, easy to scale |
| HTTP methods | Standard REST verbs | Predictable, self-documenting API |
| Status codes | 200/201/204/400/404/409 | Semantically correct per REST spec |
| Response shape | `{ success, data, error }` | Consistent client-side parsing |
| Validation | Pre-route helper function | Fails fast before any business logic |

This implementation gives you a complete, runnable API that demonstrates professional-grade patterns while remaining accessible for learning. Each file is self-contained with explanations so you can understand the architecture at every level.

# REST API for Book Management — Full Implementation

## Overview

This project implements a complete CRUD REST API for managing books using **Node.js** and **Express.js**. The architecture follows a layered pattern: **routes → services → data layer**, which separates concerns and makes the code testable and maintainable. We use an in-memory array as storage (reset on every restart) so you can run it without any database setup. Every function is documented with JSDoc, errors are handled through custom middleware, and each endpoint includes inline curl examples for quick testing.

---

## File 1: `package.json`

```json
{
  "name": "book-rest-api",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "A simple REST API for managing books with full CRUD operations and statistics",
  "main": "src/app.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node src/app.js",
    "dev": "nodemon src/app.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "express": "^4.18.2"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "nodemon": "^3.0.0"
  }
}
```

> **Why this structure?** `package.json` declares our single runtime dependency (`express`) and a dev dependency (`nodemon`) for hot-reloading during development. The `"start"` script runs the app directly; `"dev"` uses nodemon to auto-restart on file changes — useful while building.

