## File 3: `errors/customErrors.js` — Custom Error Classes

```javascript
/**
 * @fileoverview Custom error classes for consistent error handling across the API.
 * 
 * WHY CUSTOM ERROR CLASSES?
 * Standard JavaScript errors (Error, TypeError) don't carry HTTP status codes or structured
 * messages. By creating a hierarchy of custom errors, we can:
 * 1. Catch specific error types in middleware and return appropriate HTTP responses.
 * 2. Provide developers with clear, actionable error messages.
 * 3. Maintain consistency in error response format across all endpoints.
 * 
 * ERROR CLASS HIERARCHY:
 * - BaseError: Parent class with statusCode and isOperational flag.
 * - ValidationError: Client sent invalid data (400 Bad Request).
 * - NotFoundError: Resource doesn't exist (404 Not Found).
 * - InternalServerError: Unexpected server-side failure (500).
 */

/**
 * Base error class for all custom API errors.
 * Extends the built-in Error class to maintain compatibility with try/catch blocks.
 */
class BaseError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Human-readable error description.
   * @param {number} statusCode - HTTP status code associated with this error.
   */
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message); // Call parent constructor to set the message property.
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    // isOperational indicates whether the error was expected (true) or a bug (false).
    // This helps distinguish between client mistakes and server bugs in logging.
    this.isOperational = true;

    // Capture stack trace for debugging while keeping the prototype chain intact.
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

/**
 * Validation error — thrown when request data fails validation checks.
 * Example: Missing required fields, invalid email format, rating out of range.
 */
class ValidationError extends BaseError {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message, 400); // HTTP 400 Bad Request
  }
}

/**
 * Not found error — thrown when a requested resource doesn't exist.
 * Example: GET /books/999 when only books 1-3 exist.
 */
class NotFoundError extends BaseError {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message, 404); // HTTP 404 Not Found
  }
}

/**
 * Internal server error — thrown for unexpected failures.
 * Example: Database connection lost, unhandled exception in business logic.
 */
class InternalServerError extends BaseError {
  constructor(message = "An unexpected error occurred") {
    super(message, 500); // HTTP 500 Internal Server Error
  }
}

// Export all error classes for use throughout the application.
module.exports = {
  BaseError,
  ValidationError,
  NotFoundError,
  InternalServerError
};
```

**Why This Pattern?**  
- **Operational vs. Programming Errors**: `isOperational` lets us log client errors at INFO level and server bugs at ERROR level — critical for monitoring.  
- **HTTP Status Codes in Errors**: Tying status codes to error types makes the middleware layer simpler and more maintainable.  
- **Extending Built-in Error**: Ensures compatibility with existing Node.js error handling while adding REST-specific properties.

